nly possible pair would be if they have two of the same tile. But in the current hand, all are single. So the user must have a pair in their hand. Wait, but the hand has five tiles. If they have a pair, that uses two tiles, leaving three tiles for a third meld. But with five tiles, it's possible to have a pair and a three-tile meld. \n\nWait, let's re-express the hand. The five tiles are W6, W7, W8, T2, T4. If the user can form a pair and a three-tile meld. For example, if they have W6 and W7 as a pair, and W8 as a single, but that doesn't form a meld. Alternatively, if they have W7 and W8 as a pair, and W6 as a single. No. Alternatively, maybe the user can form a pair with T2 and T4? No. So the only way is to have a three-tile meld and a pair. But the three-tile meld would have to be a Chow or a Pung. \n\nThe only possible three-tile meld is the W6-W7-W8 Chow. If the user forms that, then the remaining tiles are T2 and T4. Since they can't form a pair, that's a problem. Therefore, maybe the user's current hand isn't a winning hand yet. So they need to draw or discard to reach a winning state. \n\nIn this case, the user is choosing to discard one of their tiles. The goal is to discard a tile that's least useful to others, while keeping the hand in a position to form a winning hand. \n\nIf the user discards W7, they have W6 and W8 left. If they draw W7 later, they can form the Chow. But if they keep W6 and W8, they need to draw W7. Alternatively, if they discard W6, they have W7 and W8, needing W6. Similarly for W8. \n\nBut since the user has to discard now, maybe the best option is to discard the middle tile, W7, because it's more likely to be needed by others for a Chi. Alternatively, if they discard W6 or W8, maybe they are less needed. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, W6 has 3 left, W7 has 3, W8 has 4. So the availability is similar. \n\nAlternatively, looking at the other tiles in hand: T2 and T4. The user has a T1-T2-T3 meld, so T2 is part of that. If the user