remaining. Since the user already has a B4 in the meld, maybe discarding B4 is safe because there's only one left, and they might not need it. Alternatively, if they keep B4, maybe they can form a pair with it if they get another B4. But there's only one left. So maybe discarding B4 is better.\n\nSo, considering all options, discarding B4 might be the best choice. It leaves the user with W6, W8, T2, T4. They can then look to form a pair or a fourth meld. For example, if they can get a T3, they could form a Chow of T2, T3, T4. But they need a T3. If they draw a T3, they can form that. Alternatively, if they can form a pair with T2 or T4 if they get another. \n\nAlternatively, if they discard W6, they have W8, T2, T4, B4. Maybe they can form a pair with B4 if they get another, but only one left. \n\nBetween the options, discarding B4 seems better because it's a tile that's already in a meld, and there's only one left. So discarding B4 is less likely to block future melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard B4, as it is part of an existing meld and has limited remaining tiles, reducing the risk of blocking future melds. Keeping W6 and W8 could help form a potential Chow if W7 is drawn, while T2 and T4 may pair with T3 if available.\nReason: Discarding B4 minimizes the risk of holding a tile with low remaining availability (only 1 left) and preserves other tiles for potential melds like a Chow (W6-W7-W8 or T2-T3-T4). This maintains flexibility for forming a winning hand with higher scoring patterns.\nAnswer: Play B4"}
