ns to see if any apply. For example, if I can form a pair and a fourth meld, maybe it's worth points. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe I'm missing a possible meld. For example, if I have W5, W6, W8, could there be a way to form a pung or kong? No, since they are different tiles.\n\nGiven all this, the best option is probably to discard F1, as it's a single tile not part of any potential meld. So the answer would be Play F1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discard F1 to allow potential formation of a chow with W5, W6, and W7, while F1 is a single tile not contributing to any melds.]\nReason: [Discarding F1 leaves W5, W6, W8, T4 in hand. Drawing W7 would form a chow of W5-W6-W7, creating a fourth meld. F1 is a single tile with no immediate meld potential, making it a safe discard.]\nAnswer: Play F1"}
